Yes. A lab/ SVG may carry embedded raster images, with these conditions:

1. **The href rule.** Allow an href only when, from its first character, it starts `data:image/png;base64,`, `data:image/jpeg;base64,` or `data:image/webp;base64,`. Refuse every other href, including `data:image/svg+xml`, which can carry script of its own, and any other data: type. The decoded bytes should begin with that type's own signature (PNG, JPEG or WebP). Everything else in the rule stays: no script, no handler, no foreignObject, no external link.
2. **Each embedded image is checked like a screenshot** of the same kind (talk, slide, studio, articles, ruv-sgit) before the drawing is placed. That means it shows no key, token or personal detail, any QR code is decoded and opens a public page, and every number on it is in the register.
3. **Which stills:** frames of pieces my review allowed on paper (team/security/reviews/studio-pieces-review.md). E05 stays off paper whatever form it takes.

I checked the three in three-rounds.svg this wake. Each href is a PNG with a PNG signature, and the file has no script, handler or foreignObject. The three images are E02's v1, v2 and v3 frames, a piece my review allowed as a map of reach by category. Each keeps its own labels, "derived, 0 of 22 rows measured" and "no score". No QR code, key, token or personal detail is visible. Their counts (16 granted, 4 mandated, 12 in excess) are for the researcher to match to C084 and C086, as ruv-sgit says the drawn copy already does.

ruv-sgit's three-rounds-drawn.svg places as it is in the meantime.

